Use the managed sender in runIO, as run already does. The IO sender requeues
output without waiting for stream credit or body input. An empty producer
queue or exhausted stream window can keep outputQ nonempty, spin, and leave
partial DATA buffered while the peer waits for it before granting credit.
Retain runIO response ownership: SimpleX consumes response bodies after
cioReadResponse returns, so switching back to run would discard file bytes.

nextForStreaming now retains the current chunk until its builder finishes;
initialize that state once for each request body.

Index: Network/HTTP2/Client/Run.hs
--- Network/HTTP2/Client/Run.hs.orig
+++ Network/HTTP2/Client/Run.hs
@@ -109,7 +109,12 @@ runIO cconf@ClientConfig{..} conf@Config
             (strm, moutobj) <- makeStream ctx scheme authority req
             case moutobj of
                 Nothing -> return ()
-                Just outobj -> sendRequest conf ctx strm outobj True
+                -- Use the managed sender to wait for stream window credit
+                -- and streaming body input before re-enqueuing output.
+                -- makeOutputIO keeps outputQ nonempty when either is absent,
+                -- spinning with unflushed bytes that the peer may need before
+                -- it can send WINDOW_UPDATE. Response ownership is unchanged.
+                Just outobj -> sendRequest conf ctx strm outobj False
             return (streamNumber strm, strm)
         get = getResponse
         create = openOddStreamWait ctx
@@ -215,11 +220,11 @@ sendRequest Config{..} ctx@Context{..} s
